`/home/server-manager/slt-evidence/SLT-SW-05-D09-order-13758.png`.

## Resolution — 2026-08-12

### False-positive assessment and root cause

The report's literal expectation that new order items should use
`_arraysubs_subscription_id` was outdated. The canonical ArraySubs item ownership key is
`_subscription_id`. The underlying defect was nevertheless real and critical:

- recurring-billing order creation did not write the canonical item ownership key;
- refund-order discovery still queried the obsolete `_arraysubs_subscription_id` key;
- shared checkout charges had no durable, exact per-subscription attribution contract;
- a refund could therefore miss the latest renewal, use an unsafe order-level inference,
  or consume the wrong subscription's refundable capacity on a shared order.

### Implemented fix

- Renewal product items, shipping, Store Credit fees, and plan-switch fees now use the
  canonical `_subscription_id` ownership contract.
- Checkout finalization persists a versioned charge-attribution envelope containing exact
  per-subscription net/tax allocations plus an explicit unowned remainder. Publication of
  the finalized action is gated on durable read-back success.
- Refund discovery now resolves the canonical latest settled order, validates the
  subscription/order relationship and customer identity, and never falls back to an older
  charge when the latest charge has no provable capacity.
- Refund processing validates line, quantity, tax, fee, shipping, discount, and credit
  ownership; uses currency minor units; reserves capacity under an order lock; records an
  idempotent per-subscription state ledger; and replays recoverable post-gateway work through
  the centralized scheduler. Ambiguous, stale, mixed-owner, cross-customer, malformed, and
  over-capacity requests fail closed.
- Core and Pro producers were aligned, including Stripe, Mollie, Store Credit,
  Subscription Shipping, Box, and Bundle. Pro child subscriptions retain relationship
  history but cannot claim the parent's charge ownership.

### Automated regression verification

The final code passed all 18 isolated WP-CLI scenarios, one at a time:

`native`, `allocation`, `free`, `precision`, `overflow`, `renewal`, `envelope`,
`negative`, `signed`, `shared-refund`, `validation`, `external`, `reservation`,
`identity-latest`, `quantity-tax`, `mixed`, `pro`, and `pro-custom`.

Coverage included native WooCommerce refund timing, full and partial refunds, ordinary
orders, free lines, shared orders, exact tax rounding, quantity allocation, signed refund
lines, over-refund rejection, replay/idempotency, concurrent reservations, guest and
cross-customer identity, latest-order selection, external gateway delegates, and all
affected Pro charge sources. Every scenario ended with zero fixture orders, refunds, order
items, subscriptions, products, notes, replay actions, locks, or busy actions.

PHP syntax checks passed for every changed PHP file in both plugins, and both repository
diff checks passed. PHPCS was intentionally skipped under the issue-fix workflow.

With ArraySubsPro temporarily inactive, the `renewal`, `envelope`, `native`, and
`allocation` scenarios also passed with zero residue. ArraySubsPro was restored and its
active state was verified immediately afterward.

A final independent blocker audit identified Action Scheduler's integer-zero failure
return as distinct from boolean `false`. The shared scheduler now normalizes that result to
`false`, and the refund caller also treats every falsey result as failure. A forced
`pre_as_schedule_single_action=0` probe returned strict `false` and created no action;
`allocation`, `reservation`, and `native` then passed again with zero residue.

Read-only checks against the original long-lived gateway fixtures also pass without
mutating them: Stripe subscription `#11959` resolves latest settled order `#13762` with a
`$10.00` maximum, and Paddle subscription `#13344` resolves renewal order `#13758` with a
`$5.00` maximum. The narrowly bounded historical-order inference requires one canonical
owner plus matching identity, relationship, and complete product-line ownership; ambiguous
or mixed historical orders remain non-refundable.

### Live browser verification

Using the real HPOS admin UI on `mirror-help.arrayhash.com`:

1. Controlled subscription `#15436` and processing order `#15439` were created for customer
   `#347` (`slt-core@example.test`).
2. The order line visibly rendered `Subscription: #15436`.
3. A full `$10.00` line-item refund was submitted through WooCommerce's Refund UI.
4. WooCommerce created refund `#15442`, changed order `#15439` to `refunded`, and rendered
   the `-$10.00` refund row.
5. Persisted refund data contained `_subscription_id=15436`, allocation
   `{15436: 10}`, valid full-order and line-item flags, and completed `note`, `action`, and
   `lifecycle` idempotency states. No invalid-context marker was present.
6. Subscription `#15436` changed from Active to Cancelled, recorded exactly `$10.00` total
   refunded, added refund/cancellation notes, and displayed refund `#15442` in Order History.
7. Mailpit captured the customer refund email
   `5kdwEDE0kJpdHqBQvVTiKW`, customer cancellation email
   `3Lvg0zxpXoi6fPX3bl07af`, and admin cancellation email
   `1iedvgRJj8NDS8xnfDovDb`.

